In 1930, the Adler Planetarium opened on the lakefront in [[@chicago|Chicago]], the first in the Americas. Many US cities followed, During the [[@space-race|space race]], NASA astronauts from the Mercury to the Apollo missions [[@astronaut-training|trained at the Morehead Planetarium]] in Chapel Hill, North Carolina. They learned to recognise stars they might need for steering if their instruments failed.

India's planetariums became famous too. The Birla Planetarium in [[@kolkata|Kolkata]], opened in 1963, is one of the largest in Asia and is shaped a little like the Sanchi Stupa. The Nehru Planetarium in Mumbai opened in 1977, and many more now operate in cities and science centres across India.

{{story:optical-illusions}}

Each wheel is about 3 metres across, taller than an adult. Each one has eight thick spokes and eight thinner ones in between, all covered in carvings of flowers, animals and people.

Many guides explain that the wheels work as sundials. The shadow of a spoke's axle falls on the rim and shows the time of day, so you can [[@time-measure|measure time]] by the Sun. Whether the builders really planned them as clocks, or simply as symbols of the Sun's cycles, historians still discuss. Either way, the idea matches the temple's subject perfectly.

{{story:sun-what}}

About 2,500 years ago, Greek thinkers linked to Pythagoras noticed that strings with [[@maths-music|simple length ratios]] sound good together. Halve a string and you get an octave, a ratio of 2 to 1. Take two-thirds of it and you get the note we call Pa when the full string plays Sa, a ratio of 3 to 2.

These ratios explain why Sa and Pa sound so restful together. For every two [[vibration|vibrations]] of Sa, Pa makes exactly three, so their waves keep lining up. Indian musicians studied these relationships closely too. The ancient *Natyashastra* describes 22 *shrutis*, tiny steps of pitch within an octave.

{{story:ears-hear}}

Rachel Carson was an American [[biologist|biologist]]. In 1962 she published *Silent Spring*, which showed how a pesticide called DDT was building up in animals and killing birds. Chemical companies attacked her, but the book made millions of people pay attention.

The USA banned most uses of DDT in 1972. The [[@bald-eagle|bald eagle]], whose eggs had been breaking, slowly recovered.

{{story:bald-eagle}}

In India, one of the most famous teachers is [[@savitribai-phule|Savitribai Phule]]. In 1848, she and her husband Jyotirao Phule started a school for girls in Pune, at a time when most girls, and many lower-caste children, were kept out of school.

She is often called India's first woman teacher. Some people threw mud and stones at her on the way to school, so she carried a second sari to change into. She kept going, and the Phules went on to open more schools.

{{story:savitribai-phule}}

In India, the Prime Minister must keep the support of a majority in the [[@lok-sabha-rajya-sabha|Lok Sabha]], the elected house of [[@parliament-india|Parliament]]. Ministers are themselves members of Parliament, and they must answer questions there. If the majority votes "no confidence", the government falls. A party can also swap its leader, so the prime minister can change without a national election.

That is what happened in Britain in 2022, when three people held the job in one year. The British Prime Minister lives at 10 Downing Street in [[@london|London]], while King Charles III is head of state.

{{story:london}}

Marco says Kublai Khan sent him on missions across China. He was amazed by its size and wealth. Hangzhou, he wrote, was the finest city in the world, with canals, bridges and markets.

What surprised him most was ordinary to the Chinese. People paid with paper notes stamped by the emperor, while Europe still used coins. They burned "black stones" for heat, which was coal. Messengers changed horses at relay stations and could carry news hundreds of kilometres in a day.

{{story:noodles-pasta}}

Flat land is rare in the mountains, so people carve hillsides into terraces, steps of flat fields held up by stone walls. They grow barley, buckwheat, potatoes, rice in lower valleys, and [[@apples|apples]] in places like Himachal and Kashmir.

Animals matter enormously. Yaks give milk, wool, meat and dung for fuel, and they carry loads at heights where horses struggle. In Ladakh and Tibet, people drink salty butter tea to keep warm. Houses are built for the cold, with thick stone or mud walls, small windows and flat roofs where grain, apricots and firewood dry in the sun. Shepherds take their flocks up to high meadows in summer and bring them down before the snows, a seasonal journey that has gone on for centuries.

{{story:chipko}}

In the 1600s, European trading companies raced to buy Indian cotton, pepper and indigo. The French East India Company arrived late. In 1674, a French official named François Martin set up a trading post at a fishing village on the Tamil coast. The village grew into a fortified town.

The Dutch captured it once, and the British captured it several times during their wars with France. Each time, it was handed back by a peace [[treaty|treaty]]. In the 1740s and 1750s, the French governor Dupleix even tried to build a French [[empire|empire]] across southern India. The British [[@east-india-company|East India Company]] beat him, and France was left with only a few small towns.

{{story:dutch-french-india}}

In the 1300s to 1500s, Hampi was the capital of the [[@vijayanagara|Vijayanagara]] empire, one of the richest kingdoms in India. Travellers from Portugal and Persia described huge markets selling gems, horses and spices. Today, its ruins are spread among enormous boulders beside the Tungabhadra river.

Visitors can see a stone chariot, temples with pillars that ring like bells when tapped, and the remains of royal baths. The empire fell after a battle in 1565, and the city was looted and left to crumble. In 1986, UNESCO listed [[@hampi|Hampi]] as a World Heritage Site. Nearby, at [[@chalukyas|Pattadakal]] and Badami, even older temples and cave shrines were carved by the Chalukya kings.

{{story:coffee}}

In 1848, Jyotirao [[@jyotirao-phule|Phule]] and his wife Savitribai opened a school for girls in Pune, at a time when very few Indian girls went to school. Savitribai became one of the first Indian women teachers. Many people were angry that girls, including girls from castes treated as low, were being taught to read.

Some threw mud at Savitribai as she walked to school, so she carried a spare sari. The Phules went on to open many more schools, and campaigned against caste discrimination and for widows' rights. Today, the University of Pune is named after Savitribai Phule, and Maharashtra celebrates her birthday, 3 January, in honour of girls' education.

{{story:ganesh-chaturthi}}

A bookcase hid the door to the rooms. During working hours the eight people had to keep almost silent, so the office workers below would not hear them. Toilets could not be flushed. Anne could not go outside, and she watched one chestnut tree through the attic window.

A few of Otto's employees, including Miep Gies, risked their lives to bring food, books and news. Anne wrote about all of it. She described arguments over meals and her crush on a boy in the annex. She wrote about her anger at her mother, her fear of bombs and her hopes of becoming a writer.

{{story:human-rights}}

Shiva and Parvati once had a special fruit, sometimes said to be a mango, and two sons who both wanted it. They set a contest: whoever went around the world three times first would win.

Kartikeya leapt onto his peacock and flew off. Ganesha, with his slow mouse, didn't even try. He walked three times around his parents instead. "You are my whole world," he said. When Kartikeya returned, the fruit was already Ganesha's. In Tamil Nadu, this story is linked to Kartikeya, called Murugan, going off to live on the hill at Palani.

{{story:storytelling-india}}

Before electric freezers, people made ice cream with [[@salt-ice-roads|ice and salt]]. In 1843, an American named Nancy Johnson in Philadelphia patented a hand-cranked freezer. A metal can of cream sat inside a wooden bucket packed with ice and salt. Turning the handle churned the cream.

Salt lowers the melting point of ice. As the salty ice melts, it soaks up heat from the cream, chilling it well below 0°C. The same science is why roads are salted in snowy parts of the USA. Today, electric [[@refrigerator|refrigeration]] does the cooling instead.

